</table>
<p class="small">Sections 1 to 10 describe the system as built. Section 11 is a proposal and is not implemented.</p>

<!-- ===================== 1. GOALS ===================== -->
<h1>1. Architecture Goals and Principles</h1>

<h2>1.1 Goals</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:24%">Goal</th><th>What it means here</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Separation of concerns</td><td>Employee data, payroll calculation and AI each live in their own service with a single responsibility.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Correctness of money</td><td>Salaries use exact decimal types in Java; payroll amounts are rounded to two decimals in one place; duplicate runs for the same month are impossible.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Safe handling of personal data</td><td>Photos are in a private bucket reached through expiring links; secrets live outside the source code; internal errors are not shown to users.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Graceful degradation</td><td>A failure in AWS or OpenAI disables only the dependent feature, not the whole application.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Replaceable parts</td><td>Each layer can be swapped (for example the data store) without changing the layers above it.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Simplicity for a demo</td><td>Everything runs locally with no containers, gateway or identity provider. The simplifications are listed in section 11.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>1.2 Principles applied</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>One owner per piece of data.</b> The employee service is the only source of employee records; other services ask it instead of keeping copies of salaries.</li>
  <li><b>Layered services.</b> Entry point (controller/routes) &rarr; business rules (service) &rarr; data access (repository). A layer only calls the one below it.</li>
  <li><b>Validate at the boundary, twice for critical rules.</b> In employee-service, format rules are annotations on the request object (DTO); rules that need other data (allowed positions, duplicate e-mail, salary range) are in the service layer; the main rules, including a unique e-mail, are repeated as database constraints.</li>
  <li><b>Fail with the right status.</b> 4xx means the caller made a mistake; 5xx means this service or one behind it failed.</li>
  <li><b>Secrets by configuration.</b> Keys and cloud settings come from git-ignored <span class="code">.env</span> files or the AWS credentials file.</li>
  <li><b>Stateless where possible.</b> The AI service stores nothing; the front end keeps only the sign-in token in session storage.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ===================== 2. CONTEXT ===================== -->
<h1>2. System Context View</h1>
<p>The context view shows the system as one box, the people who use it and the outside systems it depends on.</p>

<table>
  <tr><td colspan="3" class="zone">People</td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="3" class="ui"><b>Payroll officer / HR administrator</b><br>Manages employees, runs payroll, reviews payslips, asks the assistant questions</td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="3" class="arrow">&#9650;&#9660; &nbsp; HTTPS-capable web browser (HTTP on localhost in this build)</td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="3" class="svc"><b>Payroll AI Capstone system</b><br>React front end + employee-service + payroll-service + ai-service</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;<br>AWS SDK</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;<br>AWS SDK</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;<br>HTTPS REST</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="ext" style="width:33%"><b>AWS S3</b><br>Private bucket<br>Employee photos</td>
    <td class="ext" style="width:33%"><b>AWS STS</b><br>Identity check<br>(AWS status button)</td>
    <td class="ext" style="width:33%"><b>OpenAI API</b><br>Payroll review<br>Chat answers</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2>2.1 External dependencies</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:18%">External system</th><th style="width:24%">Used by</th><th>Purpose</th><th style="width:22%">If unavailable</th></tr>
  <tr><td>AWS S3</td><td>payroll-service</td><td>Store, list, delete and sign employee photos.</td><td>Photos unavailable; avatars fall back to initials.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>AWS STS</td><td>payroll-service</td><td>Show which AWS identity is connected (masked).</td><td>Status shows an error; nothing else affected.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>OpenAI API</td><td>ai-service</td><td>Review payroll records; generate chat answers.</td><td>Review falls back to fixed rules; chat reports an error.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================== 3. CONTAINERS ===================== -->
<h1>3. Container View</h1>
<p>A <i>container</i> here means a separately running, separately deployable unit. There are four, plus the stores each one uses.</p>

<h2>3.1 Container diagram</h2>
<table>
  <tr><td colspan="5" class="ui"><b>React single-page application</b> &nbsp;|&nbsp; Vite dev server &nbsp;|&nbsp; port 5173<br>Sign in / sign up / reset &bull; employee directory &bull; payroll form and history &bull; payslip viewer &bull; AI results &bull; chat widget</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="arrow" style="width:20%">&#9650;&#9660;<br><span class="small">JSON/HTTP</span></td>
    <td class="gap" style="width:5%"></td>
    <td class="arrow" style="width:50%">&#9650;&#9660; &nbsp; <span class="small">JSON/HTTP, raw bytes for photos</span></td>
    <td class="gap" style="width:5%"></td>
    <td class="arrow" style="width:20%">&#9650;&#9660;<br><span class="small">JSON/HTTP</span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="svc"><b>employee-service</b><br>Java 21<br>Spring Boot 4.1.1<br>port 8081<br><span class="small">employees + sign-in</span></td>
    <td class="arrow">&#9668;</td>
    <td class="svc"><b>payroll-service</b><br>Node.js, Express 5<br>port 3001</td>
    <td class="arrow">&#9658;</td>
    <td class="svc"><b>ai-service</b><br>Python, FastAPI<br>port 8000</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="arrow">&#9660;<br><span class="small">JDBC</span></td>
    <td class="gap"></td>
    <td class="arrow">&#9660;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&#9660;<br><span class="small">file I/O &nbsp;&nbsp; AWS SDK</span></td>
    <td class="gap"></td>
    <td class="arrow">&#9660;<br><span class="small">HTTPS</span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="store">H2 in-memory DB<br><span class="small">employees, users, password_resets</span></td>
    <td class="gap"></td>
    <td class="store">payroll-runs.json &nbsp;|&nbsp; AWS S3 bucket<br><span class="small">history &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; photos</span></td>
    <td class="gap"></td>
    <td class="ext">OpenAI API</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<p class="small">Arrows between the three services: payroll-service calls employee-service (&#9668;); ai-service also reads employee-service and payroll-service for the chat (&#9658; and a second link to employee-service, listed in 3.2).</p>

<h2>3.2 Container-to-container connections</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:25%">From &rarr; To</th><th style="width:17%">Protocol</th><th>Calls</th><th style="width:20%">Triggered by</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Browser &rarr; employee-service</td><td>HTTP/JSON</td><td><span class="code">GET/POST/DELETE /api/employees</span> (token; writes need admin) and <span class="code">/api/auth/signup, login, forgot-password, reset-password, me, logout</span></td><td>Directory actions; every sign-in screen</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>Browser &rarr; payroll-service</td><td>HTTP/JSON, raw bytes</td><td>Payroll, history, photos, AWS status</td><td>Payroll form, photo button, status button</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>Browser &rarr; ai-service</td><td>HTTP/JSON</td><td><span class="code">POST /api/ai/analyze</span>, <span class="code">POST /api/ai/chat</span></td><td>Analyze button, chat widget</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>payroll-service &rarr; employee-service</td><td>HTTP/JSON</td><td><span class="code">GET /api/employees/{id}</span> with the caller's token. (The token itself is verified locally; there is no call per request.)</td><td>Running payroll; saving a photo</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>ai-service &rarr; employee-service</td><td>HTTP/JSON (5 s timeout)</td><td><span class="code">GET /api/employees</span> with the user's token. (The token itself is verified locally.)</td><td>Each chat question</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>ai-service &rarr; payroll-service</td><td>HTTP/JSON (5 s timeout)</td><td><span class="code">GET /api/payroll</span> with the user's token</td><td>Each chat question</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>ai-service &rarr; OpenAI</td><td>HTTPS (30 s timeout, 1 retry)</td><td>Chat completions</td><td>Analyze, chat</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>8</td><td>payroll-service &rarr; AWS</td><td>HTTPS via AWS SDK</td><td>S3 put/get/list/delete, STS identity</td><td>Photo actions, status button</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>3.3 Container responsibilities at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:20%">Container</th><th>Owns</th><th style="width:30%">Does not do</th></tr>
  <tr><td>React front end</td><td>Presentation, input hints, sign-in screens, holding the sign-in token, showing or hiding controls by role</td><td>Business rules, storage of business data, security enforcement (the services enforce roles themselves)</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>employee-service</td><td>Employee records and their validation; employee code generation; <b>user accounts, password hashes, reset codes, the sign-in endpoints and issuing the signed JWT tokens</b></td><td>Payroll, photos, AI</td></tr>
  <tr><td>payroll-service</td><td>Pay calculation, deductions, payroll history, CSV export, photos, AWS status</td><td>Employee master data (it asks for it)</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>ai-service</td><td>Rules and OpenAI review, chat logic, assembling the chat data snapshot</td><td>Storing anything</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================== 4. COMPONENTS ===================== -->
<h1>4. Component Views</h1>

<!-- 4.1 frontend -->
<h2>4.1 Front end (React)</h2>
<p>The front end is a single-page app in one source file, <span class="code">src/App.jsx</span>, with styles in <span class="code">App.css</span>. It has no router; screens are sections of one page and pop-ups are controlled by state.</p>

<table>
  <tr><td class="layer" colspan="3">Root &nbsp;(reads <span class="code">sessionStorage["payday-session"]</span>)</td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="3" class="arrow">&#9660; logged out &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &#9660; logged in</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="layer" style="width:30%">Login<br><span class="small">sign in / sign up / forgot / reset</span></td>
    <td class="layer" style="width:40%">App (dashboard)<br><span class="small">state, forms, tables, pop-ups</span></td>
    <td class="layer" style="width:30%">ChatWidget<br><span class="small">own conversation state</span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr><td class="gap"></td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="gap"></td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="gap"></td>
    <td class="layer">Payslip &nbsp;|&nbsp; Icon &nbsp;|&nbsp; <span class="code">request()</span> helper</td>
    <td class="gap"></td>
  </tr>
</table>

<table>
  <tr><th style="width:24%">Component</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">Root</span></td><td>Decides between the sign-in screens and the app. Keeps the token, expiry, user and role in session storage, which is cleared when the tab closes; signs out when the expiry passes or any service answers 401. Sign-out just forgets the token, because there is no server session to end.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">Login</span></td><td>One component with four modes (sign in, sign up, forgot password, reset password). Checks the fields in the browser first, then calls employee-service <span class="code">/api/auth/...</span>; shows a message under each field that has a problem and clears the password after a failure.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">App</span></td><td>Holds employees, payroll runs, photos, form state, busy flags, errors and pop-up state. Loads data from all three services, validates forms (salary range by position, +63 contact number, @pwc.com email) and renders the directory, payroll form, breakdown, history, AI results and pop-ups (delete confirmation, payslip).</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">Payslip</span></td><td>Shared layout for the pay breakdown shown after calculation and in the history viewer. Older runs without tax details show a shorter version.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">ChatWidget</span></td><td>Floating button that opens a chat panel; sends the full conversation to ai-service on each question and shows the answer or an error.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">request()</span></td><td>One wrapper around <span class="code">fetch</span>: parses JSON, turns non-OK responses into error messages using the service's <span class="code">error</span> or <span class="code">detail</span> field, and reports an unreachable service clearly.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Constants</td><td><span class="code">API</span> (the three base URLs), <span class="code">SALARY_RANGES</span> and photo limits (types, 2 MB) mirror the back-end rules for early feedback; the back end remains the authority.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note"><b>State approach:</b> React <span class="code">useState</span> hooks only. The browser holds no business data beyond what it has just fetched.</div>

<!-- 4.2 employee-service -->
<h2>4.2 employee-service (Java / Spring Boot)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><td class="layerg">EmployeeController &nbsp;<span class="small">HTTP, status codes, CORS</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="layerg">EmployeeService &nbsp;<span class="small">validation, clean-up, salary rules</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="layerg">EmployeeRepository &nbsp;<span class="small">SQL via JdbcTemplate</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="store">H2 in-memory database &nbsp;<span class="small">schema.sql creates the table; data.sql loads 10 sample rows</span></td></tr>
</table>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:24%">Component</th><th>Responsibility</th><th style="width:26%">Depends on</th></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">EmployeeServiceApplication</span></td><td>Entry point; <span class="code">@SpringBootApplication</span> enables auto-configuration and component scanning of the sub-packages.</td><td>Spring Boot</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">EmployeeController</span></td><td>Maps four endpoints; converts JSON to <span class="code">CreateEmployee</span>; returns 200, 201, 204, 400, 404; allows CORS from port 5173.</td><td>EmployeeService</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">EmployeeService</span></td><td>Adds the rules that need other data to the format errors found by the DTO annotations: position must be an allowed value, e-mail must not already exist (case-insensitive), salary must fit the position's range (exact decimal comparison). Returns one message per field, in form order. Lower-cases the e-mail before saving.</td><td>EmployeeRepository</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">EmployeeRepository</span></td><td>The only SQL: select all, select by id, insert (returns generated id), check whether an e-mail already exists, delete. Uses bound parameters.</td><td>JdbcTemplate</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">AuthController</span></td><td>Sign-up, login, forgot-password, reset-password, me and logout endpoints under <span class="code">/api/auth</span>; one message per field for invalid input; turns <span class="code">AuthException</span> into 400, 401, 409 or 429.</td><td>AuthService</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">AuthService</span></td><td>Account rules: sign-up always creates a member; login checks the hash (against a dummy hash for unknown e-mails) and counts failures, then asks <span class="code">JwtService</span> for a token; creates and verifies one-time 6-digit reset codes (written to the log). Nothing about the token is stored.</td><td>AuthRepository, PasswordHasher, AttemptTracker, JwtService</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">JwtService</span></td><td>Issues and verifies the token: a JWT signed with HS256 whose claims are the user id (subject), e-mail, role, issuer, audience, issued-at and a 60-minute expiry. Verification accepts only HS256, checks the signature, the expiry, the issuer and audience, and that subject, e-mail and role exist; any failure returns "nobody". Refuses to start if <span class="code">JWT_SECRET</span> is under 32 characters.</td><td>Nimbus JOSE + JWT</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">AuthRepository</span></td><td>SQL for <span class="code">users</span> and <span class="code">password_resets</span>.</td><td>JdbcTemplate</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">PasswordHasher</span></td><td>PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512, 210,000 iterations, random 16-byte salt per password; constant-time comparison; the stored text records its own settings.</td><td>JDK crypto</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">AttemptTracker</span></td><td>In-memory count of failed attempts per key (5 failures lock for 15 minutes); bounded to 10,000 keys so it cannot be used to exhaust memory.</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">AuthInterceptor</span> / <span class="code">WebConfig</span></td><td>The doorman for <span class="code">/api/employees</span>: no valid token gives 401; a non-read request from a non-admin gives 403; browser pre-flight requests pass.</td><td>AuthService</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">AdminSeeder</span></td><td>Creates the first administrator at startup (hashing its password in code); a no-op if it already exists.</td><td>AuthRepository, PasswordHasher</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">Employee</span> (record)</td><td>Response shape: id, employeeCode, fullName, position, emailAddress, contactNumber, baseSalary.</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">User</span> / <span class="code">UserAccount</span> / <span class="code">LoginResult</span></td><td>The user shown to clients (id, e-mail, role), the internal record that includes the password hash (never returned), and the sign-in answer (token, expiry, user).</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">CreateEmployee</span> (record)</td><td>Request shape and format validation, written as Bean Validation annotations: required fields, length limits, name letters and spaces only (a period allowed only in a final Jr. or Sr.), @pwc.com e-mail pattern, +63 phone pattern, salary above 0 with at most 2 decimal places. A compact constructor trims the text fields. Excludes id and code so clients cannot choose them.</td><td>-</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">application.properties</span></td><td>Port 8081, H2 in-memory URL, SQL initialisation on every start. The H2 web console is switched off because it has no login and would expose the users table.</td><td>-</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- 4.3 payroll-service -->
<h2>4.3 payroll-service (Node.js / Express)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><td class="layerg" colspan="3"><span class="code">server.js</span> &rarr; <span class="code">src/app.js</span> &nbsp;<span class="small">cors &rarr; JSON parser (32 KB) &rarr; routes &rarr; error handler</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="3" class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="layerg" colspan="3">routes/ &nbsp;<span class="small">health &bull; payroll &bull; aws &bull; employee-images (raw body, 2 MB)</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="3" class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="layerg" style="width:33%">payroll.controller</td>
    <td class="layerg" style="width:33%">image.controller</td>
    <td class="layerg" style="width:34%">aws.controller</td>
  </tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="layerg">payroll.service<br><span class="small">+ ph-deductions</span></td>
    <td class="layerg">image.service</td>
    <td class="layerg">aws.service</td>
  </tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660; &nbsp;&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660; &nbsp;&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="store">payroll.repository<br><span class="small">JSON file + write queue</span><br>employee.client</td>
    <td class="store">image.repository<br><span class="small">S3</span><br>employee.client</td>
    <td class="store">AWS STS</td>
  </tr>
</table>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:26%">Component</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">config/index.js</span></td><td>Loads <span class="code">.env</span> from an absolute path, so it works from any start folder. Exposes port, employee-service URL, CORS origin, body limits, AWS settings, image limits (2 MB, 300 s link life).</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">app.js</span></td><td>Builds the app; middleware order is fixed so the error handler catches everything above it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">middleware/auth.js</span> / <span class="code">routes/index.js</span></td><td><span class="code">requireUser</span> is placed in front of everything under <span class="code">/api</span> (not <span class="code">/health</span>): it reads the bearer token and verifies the JWT locally with <span class="code">jsonwebtoken</span> and the shared secret (HS256 only; expiry, issuer and audience checked), then stores the user and token on the request. 401 for a missing, forged, tampered or expired token. There is no call to employee-service. <span class="code">requireAdmin</span> is added to running payroll, photo upload/delete and the AWS identity check (403 for members). Pre-flight requests pass.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">routes/image.routes.js</span></td><td>Reads photo bodies as raw bytes up to the limit; turns an over-size body into a clear 413.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">payroll.controller</span></td><td>Maps service results and errors to responses: known errors keep their status; network failures become 502; unknown errors reach the 500 handler.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">payroll.service</span></td><td>Validates the request, fetches the salary, computes gross pay (salary + salary/160 x 1.25 x overtime hours), calls <span class="code">ph-deductions</span>, rejects deductions above gross pay, builds the stored record and the CSV export.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">ph-deductions.js</span></td><td>SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG and BIR withholding tax rules and the order they apply (contributions first, then tax on gross minus contributions).</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">employee.client.js</span></td><td>Single outbound call for employee data; forwards the caller's token (employee-service now requires one); 404 stays 404, 401 stays 401, any other failure becomes 502.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">payroll.repository.js</span></td><td>Reads the JSON file (missing file = empty history; corrupt file = error); safe save through a temporary file and rename; a promise queue runs writes one at a time; the duplicate check and insert share one queued step.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">image.service.js</span></td><td>Validates the id, confirms AWS and bucket are configured, identifies JPEG/PNG/WebP from the first bytes, confirms the employee exists, hides S3 error detail behind a 503 while logging it.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">image.repository.js</span></td><td>All S3 operations; client created on first use; keys are <span class="code">employees/{id}/photo</span>; listing follows continuation tokens; signed GET links.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">aws.service.js</span> / <span class="code">aws.controller.js</span></td><td>Offline response when AWS is off; otherwise an STS identity lookup with the account reduced to its last four digits and user/role names masked.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">middleware/error-handler.js</span>, <span class="code">utils/http-error.js</span></td><td>Generic 500 that logs the real message server-side only; an error type that carries an HTTP status.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- 4.4 ai-service -->
<h2>4.4 ai-service (Python / FastAPI)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><td class="layerg" colspan="2"><span class="code">app/main.py</span> &nbsp;<span class="small">FastAPI app, CORS (GET/POST, Content-Type), routers</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="2" class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="layerg" style="width:50%">api/analysis.py &nbsp;<span class="small">POST /api/ai/analyze</span></td><td class="layerg" style="width:50%">api/chat.py &nbsp;<span class="small">POST /api/ai/chat</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="layerg">schemas/payroll.py<br><span class="small">AnalyzeRequest, PayrollRecord</span></td><td class="layerg">schemas/chat.py<br><span class="small">ChatRequest, ChatMessage</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="layerg">services/analysis.py<br><span class="small">3 rules + OpenAI result</span></td><td class="layerg">services/chat.py<br><span class="small">prompt + data + history</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="layerg">services/openai_review.py</td><td class="layerg">services/data_source.py &nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp; openai_client.py</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; &#9660;</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="ext">OpenAI (via shared client)</td><td class="store">employee-service + payroll-service &nbsp;&nbsp;|&nbsp;&nbsp; OpenAI</td></tr>
</table>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:26%">Component</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">core/auth.py</span></td><td>A FastAPI dependency that reads the bearer token and verifies the JWT locally with PyJWT and the shared secret (HS256 only; expiry, subject, issuer and audience required). Any failure gives 401; there is no call to employee-service. The analyze router applies it to every route; the chat route receives the validated token and passes it on when loading data. It does not check the role: any signed-in user may use review and chat.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">core/config.py</span></td><td>Loads <span class="code">.env</span> by absolute path; holds the key, model (default gpt-4o-mini), timeouts, batch size (50), data URLs, chat limits (12 messages, 1,000 characters each, 200 runs).</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">api/analysis.py</span>, <span class="code">api/chat.py</span></td><td>Accept a plain dictionary and validate it by hand so every bad request becomes one simple 400; map chat errors to their own 502 or 503.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">schemas/payroll.py</span></td><td>Six numeric fields with bounds; unknown fields kept (such as the id); infinite and NaN values rejected; extra top-level keys forbidden.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">schemas/chat.py</span></td><td>Roles limited to <i>user</i> and <i>assistant</i> (a caller cannot inject a system message); length limits; last message must be from the user.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">services/openai_client.py</span></td><td>Shared lazily created OpenAI client and the "is a key configured" check.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">services/openai_review.py</span></td><td>System prompt holding the payroll formulas; strict JSON-schema reply (index, flagged, reason); temperature 0; batches of 50; logs only the error type and status.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">services/analysis.py</span></td><td>Runs the three fixed rules, asks OpenAI, merges: status is <i>review</i> if the AI flagged it or any rule fired. If OpenAI fails the rule results are still returned with an <span class="code">ai_available</span> flag.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">services/data_source.py</span></td><td>Fetches live employees and runs, keeps only whitelisted fields, computes counts and totals in code, notes unavailable sections, trims to the newest 200 runs, raises an error only if both sources fail.</td></tr>
  <tr><td><span class="code">services/chat.py</span></td><td>Builds the message list (rules, data, history), calls OpenAI with temperature 0 and a 500-token cap, converts failures to 502 or 503.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td><span class="code">api/health.py</span></td><td>Reports <span class="code">openai</span> or <span class="code">rules-only</span> depending on whether a key is present.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================== 5. DATA ===================== -->
<h1>5. Data Architecture</h1>

<h2>5.1 Where data lives</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:20%">Data</th><th style="width:22%">Store</th><th style="width:16%">Owner</th><th>Lifetime</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Employee records</td><td>H2 in-memory database</td><td>employee-service</td><td>Until the service restarts, then reset to 10 sample rows</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Payroll runs</td><td><span class="code">payroll-service/data/payroll-runs.json</span></td><td>payroll-service</td><td>Persistent on disk</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Employee photos</td><td>AWS S3 bucket (private)</td><td>payroll-service</td><td>Persistent until deleted</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Sign-in token (JWT)</td><td>Browser session storage only; the server stores nothing</td><td>front end</td><td>Until the tab closes or the token expires (60 minutes)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>User accounts, reset codes</td><td>H2 in-memory database (<span class="code">users</span>, <span class="code">password_resets</span>)</td><td>employee-service</td><td>Until the service restarts; only the seeded admin is recreated</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>JWT signing secret</td><td><span class="code">JWT_SECRET</span> in each service's <span class="code">.env</span></td><td>All three services (the same value)</td><td>Until changed; changing it invalidates every token</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Chat conversation</td><td>Browser memory</td><td>front end</td><td>Until the page reloads</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Secrets and settings</td><td><span class="code">.env</span> files; AWS credentials file</td><td>Each service</td><td>Local files, git-ignored</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.2 Employee table (H2)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:20%">Column</th><th style="width:22%">Type</th><th>Constraints and behaviour</th></tr>
  <tr><td>id</td><td>BIGINT</td><td>Primary key, generated by identity</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>employee_code</td><td>VARCHAR(32)</td><td>NOT NULL, UNIQUE. Default is <span class="code">'EMP'</span> + the next value of <span class="code">employee_code_seq</span> left-padded to three digits. The sequence starts at 11 because the sample rows use EMP001 to EMP010.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>full_name</td><td>VARCHAR(160)</td><td>NOT NULL</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>position</td><td>VARCHAR(120)</td><td>NOT NULL; CHECK in (Associate, Senior Associate, Manager)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>email_address</td><td>VARCHAR(254)</td><td>NOT NULL; CHECK ends with <span class="code">@pwc.com</span>; CHECK stored value is lower case; <b>UNIQUE</b> (<span class="code">uq_employees_email</span>), so no two employees can share an e-mail. Because the service lower-cases before saving, uniqueness is case-insensitive.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>contact_number</td><td>VARCHAR(16)</td><td>NOT NULL; CHECK starts with <span class="code">+63</span></td></tr>
  <tr><td>base_salary</td><td>DECIMAL(15,2)</td><td>NOT NULL; CHECK greater than 0; table-level CHECK <span class="code">chk_salary_by_position</span> enforces the range for each position (Associate 20,000-60,000; Senior Associate 60,001-100,000; Manager above 100,000)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The service layer enforces the same rules with friendlier messages, so the database acts as a second line of defence against any writer that bypasses the service.</p>

<h2>5.3 Payroll run record (JSON)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:26%">Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
  <tr><td>id</td><td><span class="code">{period}-{employeeId}-{timestamp}</span>, for example <span class="code">2026-10-3-1792621800428</span></td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>employeeId, employeeCode, fullName</td><td>Reference to the employee plus a <b>copy</b> of their code and name taken at run time, so history still reads correctly if the employee is deleted</td></tr>
  <tr><td>period</td><td><span class="code">YYYY-MM</span></td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>baseSalary, overtimeHours, overtimePay, grossPay</td><td>Earnings, rounded to two decimals</td></tr>
  <tr><td>sss, philhealth, pagibig</td><td>Mandatory contributions (employee share)</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>taxableIncome, withholdingTax</td><td>Gross pay minus contributions; BIR tax on that amount</td></tr>
  <tr><td>otherDeductions</td><td>Extra amount entered by the user</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>deductions</td><td>Total of contributions, tax and other deductions</td></tr>
  <tr><td>netPay, createdAt</td><td>Gross pay minus deductions; ISO timestamp</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Runs saved before the tax feature lack the contribution and tax fields; the payslip viewer and the CSV export handle that.</p>

<h2>5.4 Photo objects (S3)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:26%">Item</th><th>Design</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Bucket</td><td>Private; no public access. Name set in <span class="code">AWS_S3_BUCKET</span>.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Key</td><td><span class="code">employees/{employeeId}/photo</span>. One object per employee; uploading again overwrites it.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Content type</td><td>Detected from the file's first bytes (JPEG, PNG or WebP), not taken from the client.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Access</td><td>Signed GET links valid for 300 seconds, generated on each list request.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>5.5 Relationships between services' data</h2>
<table>
  <tr><td class="store" style="width:30%"><b>employees</b> (H2)<br>id, code, name, salary...</td>
  <td class="arrow" style="width:40%">&#9668;&#8212;&#8212; referenced by value (employeeId) &#8212;&#8212;&#9658;</td>
  <td class="store" style="width:30%"><b>payroll runs</b> (JSON)<br>employeeId + copy of code and name</td></tr>
  <tr><td class="arrow">&#9650;</td><td class="arrow">referenced by value</td><td class="arrow"></td></tr>
  <tr><td class="store"><b>employee photo</b> (S3)<br>key contains employeeId</td><td class="gap"></td><td class="gap"></td></tr>
</table>
<div class="warn"><b>No cross-service integrity.</b> There are no foreign keys between services. Because employee IDs restart at 1 whenever employee-service restarts, an old payroll run or photo can point at a different person. The employee deletion flow in the UI removes the photo but does not touch payroll runs. A persistent employee database (section 11) resolves this.</div>

<h2>5.6 Data sent to OpenAI</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:16%">Feature</th><th>Leaves the machine</th><th style="width:30%">Does not leave</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Review</td><td>An index and six numbers per record: baseSalary, overtimeHours, overtimePay, grossPay, deductions, netPay</td><td>Names, employee codes, ids</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Chat</td><td>Counts and totals; for each employee: code, name, position, e-mail, contact number, salary; for each of up to 200 runs the pay fields; the conversation</td><td>Record ids, photos, credentials</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================== 6. INTERFACES ===================== -->
<h1>6. Interface and Error Contracts</h1>

<h2>6.1 Key payloads</h2>
<h3>Add employee</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:50%">Request <span class="code">POST /api/employees</span></th><th>Response <span class="code">201</span></th></tr>
  <tr><td class="code">{ "fullName": "Dani Flores",<br>&nbsp; "position": "Associate",<br>&nbsp; "emailAddress": "dani@pwc.com",<br>&nbsp; "contactNumber": "+639171112222",<br>&nbsp; "baseSalary": 28000 }</td>
  <td class="code">{ "id": 11, "employeeCode": "EMP011",<br>&nbsp; "fullName": "Dani Flores",<br>&nbsp; "position": "Associate",<br>&nbsp; "emailAddress": "dani@pwc.com",<br>&nbsp; "contactNumber": "+639171112222",<br>&nbsp; "baseSalary": 28000.00 }</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Run payroll</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:42%">Request <span class="code">POST /api/payroll/run</span></th><th>Response <span class="code">201</span> (abridged)</th></tr>
  <tr><td class="code">{ "employeeId": 1,<br>&nbsp; "period": "2026-10",<br>&nbsp; "overtimeHours": 8,<br>&nbsp; "deductions": 0 }</td>
  <td class="code">{ "id": "2026-10-1-...", "employeeCode": "EMP001",<br>&nbsp; "grossPay": 31875, "sss": 1500,<br>&nbsp; "philhealth": 750, "pagibig": 200,<br>&nbsp; "taxableIncome": 29425, "withholdingTax": 1288.8,<br>&nbsp; "deductions": 3738.8, "netPay": 28136.2, ... }</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>AI review and chat</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:42%">Request</th><th>Response</th></tr>
  <tr><td class="code">POST /api/ai/analyze<br>{ "records": [ { "id": "...", "baseSalary": 42000, ... } ] }</td>
  <td class="code">{ "count": 1, "results": [ { "id": "...", "status": "normal",<br>&nbsp; "ai_flagged": false, "ai_reason": "...", "warnings": [] } ],<br>&nbsp; "ai_available": true, "ai_error": null, "notice": "..." }</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td class="code">POST /api/ai/chat<br>{ "messages": [ { "role": "user", "content": "Who has the highest salary?" } ] }</td>
  <td class="code">{ "answer": "..." }</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>6.2 Error model</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:9%">Status</th><th style="width:26%">Meaning</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr>
  <tr><td>400</td><td>Invalid input</td><td>Field validation in all services; invalid photo type or id; deductions greater than gross pay; bad AI request</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>404</td><td>Not found</td><td>Unknown employee, payroll record or photo employee</td></tr>
  <tr><td>409</td><td>Conflict</td><td>A payroll run already exists for that employee and month</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>413</td><td>Too large</td><td>Photo over 2 MB</td></tr>
  <tr><td>422</td><td>Not a JSON object</td><td>FastAPI rejects a body that is not an object (for example <span class="code">[]</span>)</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>500</td><td>Unexpected error</td><td>Caught by the payroll-service handler: generic message to the user, real message in the server log</td></tr>
  <tr><td>502</td><td>A service behind this one failed</td><td>payroll-service cannot reach or gets an error from employee-service; ai-service cannot load data or OpenAI fails</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>503</td><td>Unavailable</td><td>Photo storage off or failing; AWS identity failing; OpenAI not configured</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Error bodies are JSON: <span class="code">{ "error": "message" }</span> from employee-service and payroll-service (for add-employee failures employee-service also returns <span class="code">fieldErrors</span>, a map of field name to message, so the form can show every error under its own input at once), and <span class="code">{ "detail": "message" }</span> from ai-service. The front end reads either.</p>

<h2>6.3 CORS</h2>
<p>All three services allow browser calls only from <span class="code">http://localhost:5173</span>. ai-service limits methods to GET and POST and headers to Content-Type, and does not allow credentials.</p>

<!-- ===================== 7. RUNTIME ===================== -->
<h1>7. Runtime Views</h1>
<p>Each table lists the interactions in order. "Browser" means the React app.</p>

<h2>7.0 Sign in and token checking</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:30%">From &rarr; To</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Browser</td><td>The sign-in form checks the fields (a @pwc.com e-mail; for sign-up, a password of 8 to 100 characters that matches the confirmation) and sends <span class="code">POST /api/auth/login</span> (or <span class="code">/signup</span>).</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>AuthController &rarr; AuthService</td><td>Annotation errors &rarr; 400 with a message per field. Sign-up creates a <i>member</i> (409 if the e-mail exists). Login: if the e-mail is locked &rarr; 429.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>AuthService</td><td>Looks up the account and checks the password against the stored hash <b>even when the account does not exist</b> (against a dummy hash), so response time does not reveal which e-mails are registered. Failure &rarr; 401 "Invalid email or password" and the failure is counted; the fifth failure locks the e-mail for 15 minutes.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>AuthService &rarr; JwtService</td><td>On success, <span class="code">JwtService</span> builds the claims (user id as subject, e-mail, role, issuer, audience, issued-at, expiry 60 minutes ahead) and signs them with HS256 and the shared secret. <b>Nothing is stored.</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>employee-service &rarr; Browser</td><td>Returns the JWT, its expiry time and the user (id, e-mail, role). The browser keeps them in session storage.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>Browser &rarr; any service</td><td>Every later call carries <span class="code">Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</span> (added in one place, the shared request helper).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>employee-service</td><td>An interceptor on <span class="code">/api/employees</span> asks <span class="code">JwtService</span> to verify the token: the signature must match, only HS256 is accepted, the expiry must be in the future, and the issuer and audience must match. Any failure &rarr; 401. A non-read request from a non-admin &rarr; 403. Browser pre-flight (OPTIONS) requests pass because they cannot carry a token.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>8</td><td>payroll-service / ai-service</td><td><b>They verify the same JWT themselves</b> with their own copy of the secret (<span class="code">jsonwebtoken</span> in Node, PyJWT in Python): same algorithm, expiry, issuer and audience checks. <b>No call to employee-service is made.</b> A bad token gives 401. payroll-service then applies <i>admin only</i> to running payroll, photo changes and the AWS status, reading the role from the token.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>9</td><td>Browser</td><td>Any 401 on a normal call clears the token and returns to the sign-in screen. The token also expires by itself at its expiry time. Signing out only deletes it from the browser.</td></tr>
</table>
<p><b>Forgot password.</b> <span class="code">POST /api/auth/forgot-password</span> always answers the same message. If the account exists, a 6-digit code is stored (fingerprint only, 15 minutes) and written to the employee-service log. <span class="code">POST /api/auth/reset-password</span> checks the code in constant time, counts wrong guesses toward the lock-out, saves the new password hash and deletes the code. Tokens already issued keep working until they expire (see the security section).</p>

<h2>7.1 Add an employee</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:30%">From &rarr; To</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Browser &rarr; Controller</td><td><span class="code">POST /api/employees</span> with the form data and the admin's token (the form already limits the salary by position and checks the e-mail and phone format). The interceptor first requires a valid token (401) and the admin role (403).</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>Controller</td><td>Empty body &rarr; 400. Otherwise passes the data to the service.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>Controller &rarr; Service</td><td>The DTO annotations have already checked the format of each field. <span class="code">validate</span> adds position, duplicate e-mail and salary-range problems. Any errors &rarr; 400 with a message for every field at once. (If two identical e-mails race past this check, the database unique rule rejects the second with a 409.)</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>Controller &rarr; Service</td><td><span class="code">create</span> trims fields and lower-cases the e-mail.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>Service &rarr; Repository &rarr; H2</td><td>INSERT without id or code; the database assigns both and returns the id.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>Repository</td><td>Reads the row back so the response includes the generated employee code.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>Controller &rarr; Browser</td><td>201 with the saved employee; the directory reloads.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.2 Run payroll</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:30%">From &rarr; To</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Browser &rarr; payroll-service</td><td><span class="code">POST /api/payroll/run</span> with employeeId, period, overtimeHours, deductions, and the admin's token. payroll-service first checks the token with employee-service and requires the admin role (401, 403, or 502 if employee-service is down).</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>payroll.service</td><td>Validates the four values; failure &rarr; 400.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>payroll-service &rarr; employee-service</td><td><span class="code">GET /api/employees/{id}</span>, forwarding the caller's token. 404 &rarr; 404; 401 &rarr; 401; other failure or unreachable &rarr; 502.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>payroll.service</td><td>Checks the returned salary is a positive number (else 502). Computes gross pay.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>ph-deductions</td><td>Computes SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG, taxable income, withholding tax and total deductions.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>payroll.service</td><td>Total deductions greater than gross pay &rarr; 400.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>payroll.repository</td><td>Inside the write queue: read the file, reject if the employee and month already exist (&rarr; 409), append the record, write a temporary file and rename it over the real one.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>8</td><td>payroll-service &rarr; Browser</td><td>201 with the record; the page shows the pay breakdown and refreshes the history.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.3 Upload an employee photo</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:30%">From &rarr; To</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Browser</td><td>Checks type and size (2 MB) before sending; sends the raw file with <span class="code">PUT /api/employee-images/{id}</span>.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>Route</td><td>Reads the body as bytes; over the limit &rarr; 413.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>image.service</td><td>Validates the id (400); AWS off or bucket missing &rarr; 503; empty body &rarr; 400; file's first bytes are not JPEG, PNG or WebP &rarr; 400.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>payroll-service &rarr; employee-service</td><td>Confirms the employee exists (404 or 502 on failure).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>image.repository &rarr; S3</td><td><span class="code">PutObject</span> to <span class="code">employees/{id}/photo</span>; failures are logged with their type and returned as 503.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>image.repository</td><td>Creates a signed link valid for 300 seconds.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>Browser</td><td>Refreshes <span class="code">GET /api/employee-images</span> (lists all photo keys page by page, signs each) and shows the photo in the avatar.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.4 AI review of payroll history</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:30%">From &rarr; To</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Browser &rarr; ai-service</td><td><span class="code">POST /api/ai/analyze</span> with the saved runs it already loaded from payroll-service, and the user's token. ai-service checks the token with employee-service first (401 or 502). Any signed-in user may run the review.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>schemas</td><td>Validates each record (positive salary and gross pay, non-negative amounts, finite numbers). Failure &rarr; 400.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>analysis.py</td><td>Computes the three fixed-rule warnings for every record.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>openai_review &rarr; OpenAI</td><td>Sends index plus six numbers per record in batches of 50; the system prompt supplies the payroll formulas; the reply must match a strict JSON schema (index, flagged, reason).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>analysis.py</td><td>Merges: <i>review</i> if flagged or any warning; else <i>normal</i>. If step 4 failed, returns the rule results with <span class="code">ai_available: false</span>.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>Browser</td><td>Shows each record's status, the AI reason and any rule warnings.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>7.5 Chat question</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:30%">From &rarr; To</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Browser &rarr; ai-service</td><td><span class="code">POST /api/ai/chat</span> with the whole conversation (max 12 messages, 1,000 characters each, last from the user) and the user's token, which ai-service validates first.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>schemas/chat</td><td>Only user and assistant roles are allowed; otherwise 400.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>chat.py</td><td>No API key &rarr; 503.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>data_source &rarr; employee-service, payroll-service</td><td>Fetches both lists (5 s timeout each) <b>with the user's own token</b>, so the chat sees only what that user may see. One missing &rarr; that section is marked unavailable. Both missing &rarr; 502.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>data_source</td><td>Keeps whitelisted fields, adds counts and totals computed in Python, trims to the newest 200 runs.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>chat.py &rarr; OpenAI</td><td>Message list: rules (system), data (system), then the conversation. Temperature 0, 500-token cap.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>chat.py</td><td>OpenAI error or refusal &rarr; 502. Otherwise returns the answer text.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>8</td><td>Browser</td><td>Appends the answer to the chat; shows an error line on failure.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">The chat reads data from the services, not from the database directly, and fetches it again on every question so answers reflect the latest records. The browser never supplies the data.</div>

<!-- ===================== 8. CROSS-CUTTING ===================== -->
<h1>8. Cross-Cutting Architecture</h1>

<h2>8.1 Security architecture</h2>
<h3>Trust zones</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:22%">Zone</th><th>Contents</th><th style="width:28%">Trust level</th></tr>
  <tr><td>User's browser</td><td>React app, sign-in token, chat</td><td>Untrusted: anything can be altered by the user, so the services re-check every request</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Application host</td><td>The three services, their files and <span class="code">.env</span></td><td>Trusted. The APIs now require a valid token, but traffic between browser and services is plain HTTP</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cloud</td><td>AWS S3/STS, OpenAI</td><td>Third parties; accessed with credentials held on the host</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Controls in place</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:26%">Control</th><th>Where and how</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Input validation</td><td>Every service validates at its edge; employee format rules are annotations on the request object, rules needing data are in the service layer, and the main employee rules (including a unique e-mail) are repeated as database constraints.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>SQL injection prevention</td><td>All SQL uses bound parameters.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Users sign in with e-mail and password. Passwords are stored only as salted PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512 hashes (210,000 iterations, random 16-byte salt) and compared in constant time. A correct sign-in returns a JWT signed with HS256 and a shared secret, valid for 60 minutes. It carries the user id, e-mail, role, issuer, audience and expiry. Its contents are readable by anyone, but cannot be changed without breaking the signature. Nothing about the token is stored on the server.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Authorisation (roles)</td><td>Two roles, <i>admin</i> and <i>member</i>, enforced by every service. employee-service: any signed-in user can read, only admins can add or delete. payroll-service: only admins can run payroll, change photos or check AWS. ai-service: any signed-in user. Sign-up always creates a member; the request has no role field.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Token checking across services</td><td>payroll-service and ai-service keep no accounts and make no call to check a token: each verifies the JWT signature, expiry, issuer and audience itself, accepting only HS256, so a token cannot choose a weaker algorithm or none. The caller's own token is forwarded when one service reads data from another, so no service holds a master key.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Shared signing secret</td><td><span class="code">JWT_SECRET</span> (at least 32 characters) is held in each service's git-ignored <span class="code">.env</span> and must be identical in all three. Each service refuses to start without it. Whoever knows it can forge an admin token, so it must never be committed or shared, and changing it signs everyone out. The <span class="code">.env.example</span> files carry the key name only, with an empty value.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Token limits (a JWT cannot be cancelled)</td><td>Signing out only clears the browser; a password reset or role change does not affect tokens already issued; a restart does not either, while the secret is unchanged. Each token works until its 60 minutes are up. Mitigations: a short lifetime, and later a revoked-token list or refresh tokens.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Brute-force protection</td><td>Five failed sign-ins (or reset-code guesses) lock that e-mail for 15 minutes (HTTP 429). The tracking table is bounded so random e-mails cannot exhaust memory. Unknown e-mails are hashed against a dummy value so timing does not reveal which accounts exist; wrong e-mail and wrong password give the same message.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Password reset</td><td>A random 6-digit code, valid 15 minutes, stored only as a fingerprint, usable once. The reset answer is identical whether or not the account exists. A reset does not cancel tokens already issued. No e-mail service exists, so the code is written to the employee-service log.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>CORS allow-list</td><td>Only <span class="code">http://localhost:5173</span> may call the services from a browser. Each service must list <span class="code">Authorization</span> among its allowed headers: a browser sends a pre-flight (OPTIONS) request before any call that carries the token, and a service that does not allow the header answers 400, so the real request is never sent. ai-service once failed this way on the chat.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Private photo storage</td><td>Bucket is private; links are signed and expire after 5 minutes.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>File type verification</td><td>Photo type is read from the file's bytes, so renaming a file does not bypass the check; 2 MB cap.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Secrets handling</td><td>OpenAI key and AWS settings in git-ignored <span class="code">.env</span> files; AWS credentials come from the AWS credentials file; keys are not logged (only error type and status).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Information hiding</td><td>Generic 500/503 messages to users; AWS identity masked; real causes go to server logs only.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Prompt-injection resistance</td><td>Chat accepts only user and assistant roles; the data and rules are supplied by the server; the system prompt tells the model to ignore instructions found in data or messages.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Cost and abuse limits</td><td>Chat length limits, 500-token answers, request timeouts, batching.</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="warn"><b>Gaps:</b> traffic is plain HTTP, so tokens could be read on a real network; a JWT cannot be cancelled, so sign-out, password reset and role changes do not affect tokens already issued; the single shared <span class="code">JWT_SECRET</span> can forge tokens for anyone and is held in three places; accounts live in memory and vanish on restart; the seeded admin password (<span class="code">admin</span>) is shorter than the 8-character minimum for new passwords; sign-up is open to any @pwc.com address without e-mail confirmation; the reset code goes to a log instead of an e-mail; the lock-out counter is per process and reset by a restart; there is no general rate limiting and no audit log. These are addressed in section 11. The OpenAI key supplied during development was pasted into a chat and should be rotated.</div>

<h2>8.2 Resilience and failure handling</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:30%">Failure</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
  <tr><td>employee-service down</td><td>Nobody can sign in. The directory fails to load. Running payroll or saving a photo returns 502. <b>Users who already hold a valid token</b> can still call payroll-service and ai-service, because those verify tokens themselves; chat still answers from payroll runs and says employees are unavailable.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>JWT_SECRET missing or under 32 characters</td><td>That service refuses to start and prints why. Fix the <span class="code">.env</span> file.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>JWT_SECRET differs between services</td><td>Sign-in works, but the service with the different value answers 401 for every call (the signatures do not match). Compare the three <span class="code">.env</span> files.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Token expires, is forged or is tampered with</td><td>The next call answers 401 and the app returns to the sign-in screen.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>payroll-service down</td><td>Payroll and history fail; photos fail; Analyze has no records to send; chat answers from employees only.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ai-service down</td><td>Analyze and chat fail with an error message; employees, payroll and photos are unaffected.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>OpenAI down or key invalid</td><td>Analyze returns rule results and flags <span class="code">ai_available: false</span>; chat returns 502 or 503.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>AWS disabled (<span class="code">AWS_ENABLED</span> not true)</td><td>Photo list returns an empty set (initials shown); upload and delete return 503; payroll is unaffected.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>S3 call fails</td><td>Cause logged as <span class="code">Image storage failed: ...</span>; user sees "Image storage unavailable"; the page falls back to initials.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Both chat data sources down</td><td>502 "Could not load employee or payroll data".</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Corrupt payroll file</td><td>Reads fail with a 500 rather than silently discarding data; a missing file is treated as empty history.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Crash during a save</td><td>The temporary-file-then-rename write means the real file is never left half written.</td></tr>
</table>
<h3>Timeouts and limits</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:36%">Item</th><th>Value</th></tr>
  <tr><td>OpenAI request timeout / retries</td><td>30 seconds / 1 retry</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>ai-service data fetch timeout</td><td>5 seconds per source</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Records per OpenAI review batch</td><td>50</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Payroll JSON body limit / photo size limit</td><td>32 KB / 2 MB</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Chat history / message length / runs given to the model / answer length</td><td>12 messages / 1,000 characters / 200 runs / 500 tokens</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Signed photo link life</td><td>300 seconds</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.3 Concurrency and consistency</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>One payroll run per employee per month.</b> The existence check and the append happen inside one step of a promise-based write queue, so simultaneous requests cannot both succeed (one gets 201, the rest 409).</li>
  <li><b>Reads wait for pending writes</b> so they never see a half-finished state.</li>
  <li><b>A failed write does not block the queue</b> (the stored queue tail swallows the error).</li>
  <li><b>Atomic file replacement</b> via temporary file and rename.</li>
  <li><b>Idempotency:</b> photo upload overwrites; photo delete succeeds even if the object is absent; payroll run is deliberately not idempotent (a repeat returns 409).</li>
  <li><b>Limit:</b> the queue lives inside one process. Two copies of payroll-service would each have their own queue and could overwrite each other.</li>
</ul>

<h2>8.4 Configuration management</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:30%">Service</th><th>Source</th><th>Items</th></tr>
  <tr><td>employee-service</td><td><span class="code">application.properties</span></td><td>Port, datasource URL, SQL init mode (H2 console off), token lifetime (<span class="code">app.jwt.ttl-minutes</span>, 60). <span class="code">employee-service/.env</span> supplies <span class="code">JWT_SECRET</span>. The first administrator is created in code at startup by <span class="code">AdminSeeder</span>, not from a SQL file.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>payroll-service</td><td><span class="code">.env</span> (absolute path), AWS credentials file</td><td>PORT, EMPLOYEE_SERVICE_URL, AWS_ENABLED, AWS_REGION, AWS_S3_BUCKET, <b>JWT_SECRET</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>ai-service</td><td><span class="code">.env</span> (absolute path)</td><td>OPENAI_API_KEY, OPENAI_MODEL, <b>JWT_SECRET</b>, optional EMPLOYEE_SERVICE_URL, PAYROLL_SERVICE_URL</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>front end</td><td>Constants in <span class="code">App.jsx</span></td><td>The three service base URLs (hard-coded to localhost)</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Both Node and Python services load <span class="code">.env</span> from a path relative to their own source files, so the start directory does not matter. Template files (<span class="code">.env.example</span>) document the expected keys.</p>

<h2>8.5 Observability</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:30%">Capability</th><th>Current state</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Health endpoints</td><td>payroll-service <span class="code">/health</span>; ai-service <span class="code">/health</span> (reports <i>openai</i> or <i>rules-only</i>). employee-service has none (no actuator).</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Diagnostic status</td><td><span class="code">/api/aws/identity</span> shows offline, connected (masked) or failing.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Startup messages</td><td>payroll-service prints whether AWS is enabled and which bucket; Uvicorn and Spring print their usual start lines.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Error logging</td><td>Console only: <span class="code">Request failed: ...</span>, <span class="code">Image storage failed: ...</span>, and OpenAI/data-source failures logged by type.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Metrics, tracing, central logs</td><td>Not implemented.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>8.6 Performance and scalability</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:24%">Component</th><th>Scalability</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  <tr><td>ai-service</td><td>Stateless; scales horizontally</td><td>Latency is dominated by the OpenAI call; each chat question refetches data and sends it all to the model.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>employee-service</td><td>Single instance only</td><td>Each instance would hold its own in-memory data. Needs a shared database to scale.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>payroll-service</td><td>Single instance only</td><td>JSON file and in-process queue; photo listing signs every photo on each page load.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>S3</td><td>Managed, scales automatically</td><td>Listing is paged.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Chat data size</td><td>Bounded</td><td>Capped at 200 runs; all employees are sent, so very large directories would need retrieval or filtering.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================== 9. DEPLOYMENT ===================== -->
<h1>9. Deployment View</h1>

<h2>9.1 Topology</h2>
<table>
  <tr><td colspan="4" class="zone">Developer / demo machine (Windows) &nbsp;-&nbsp; everything runs as separate local processes</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="ui" style="width:25%"><b>Browser</b><br>localhost:5173<br><span class="small">Vite dev server</span></td>
    <td class="svc" style="width:25%"><b>employee-service</b><br>localhost:8081<br><span class="small">JVM (mvn spring-boot:run)</span></td>
    <td class="svc" style="width:25%"><b>payroll-service</b><br>localhost:3001<br><span class="small">node server.js</span></td>
    <td class="svc" style="width:25%"><b>ai-service</b><br>localhost:8000<br><span class="small">uvicorn in a Python venv</span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" class="arrow">&#9660; outbound internet (HTTPS) from payroll-service and ai-service only</td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="2" class="ext"><b>AWS</b> (S3, STS) &nbsp; <span class="small">credentials from the AWS credentials file</span></td><td colspan="2" class="ext"><b>OpenAI API</b> &nbsp; <span class="small">key from ai-service/.env</span></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>9.2 Prerequisites and start order</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:22%">Component</th><th>Needs</th><th>Start order and notes</th></tr>
  <tr><td>employee-service</td><td>Java 21, Maven</td><td>Start first: payroll and chat depend on it. No external dependencies.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>payroll-service</td><td>Node.js, <span class="code">npm install</span>, <span class="code">.env</span>, AWS credentials (only for photos)</td><td>After employee-service. Startup does not fail if employee-service is down; calls fail later with 502.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>ai-service</td><td>Python 3.11/3.12 venv, <span class="code">pip install -r requirements.txt</span>, <span class="code">.env</span> with the OpenAI key</td><td>Any time; it contacts the other services only when a chat question is asked.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>front end</td><td>Node.js, <span class="code">npm install</span></td><td>Last. Sign in as the seeded administrator (<span class="code">vance.albert.reyes@pwc.com</span>) or sign up as a member. All three back-end services must be running for every feature to work, and employee-service must be up for anyone to sign in.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>9.3 Dependency matrix</h2>
<p>X means the row's feature needs that component to work.</p>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:26%">Feature</th><th>employee-service</th><th>payroll-service</th><th>ai-service</th><th>AWS</th><th>OpenAI</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Employee directory</td><td>X</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sign in / sign up / reset password</td><td>X</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Run payroll / history</td><td>X</td><td>X</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
  <tr><td>Employee photos</td><td>X (upload)</td><td>X</td><td></td><td>X</td><td></td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>AI review</td><td>X (checks the token)</td><td>X (supplies records)</td><td>X</td><td></td><td>optional (rules still work)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Chat assistant</td><td>X (token, employees)</td><td>partial (payroll runs)</td><td>X</td><td></td><td>X</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>9.4 Environment</h2>
<ul>
  <li>No containers, orchestration, reverse proxy or TLS. All traffic between processes is plain HTTP on localhost.</li>
  <li>Source layout: one folder per component at the repository root (<span class="code">employee-service</span>, <span class="code">payroll-service</span>, <span class="code">ai-service</span>, <span class="code">frontend</span>) plus <span class="code">docs</span>.</li>
  <li>A leftover file <span class="code">ai-service/app/services/model.py</span> (the former offline scikit-learn model) is still in the repository. Nothing imports it, but it still depends on scikit-learn, which is no longer in <span class="code">requirements.txt</span>. It can be deleted.</li>
</ul>

<!-- ===================== 10. DECISIONS ===================== -->
<h1>10. Architecture Decisions and Trade-offs</h1>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:4%">#</th><th style="width:22%">Decision</th><th style="width:32%">Why</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Three services in three languages</td><td>Clear ownership; use the strongest ecosystem for each job (Java for validated data, Node for I/O and cloud, Python for AI).</td><td>More tooling to install and run; three styles to maintain.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>Browser calls each service directly (no gateway)</td><td>Simplest possible wiring for a demo.</td><td>CORS on every service; each service has to check the token itself; no single place for TLS or rate limiting.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>Layered structure in every service</td><td>Each layer has one job and can change independently; easy to explain.</td><td>A few extra files for a small codebase.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>Plain JDBC (<span class="code">JdbcTemplate</span>) instead of JPA</td><td>Visible, simple SQL; fewer moving parts.</td><td>Manual row mapping; more SQL to maintain as the model grows.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>H2 in-memory for employees</td><td>Zero setup; sample data on every start.</td><td>Data lost on restart; IDs reset; cannot scale out.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>Format rules as DTO annotations; data-dependent rules in the service; main rules also in the database</td><td>Each rule sits next to the field it protects; friendly per-field messages; a safety net against other writers and against two simultaneous identical e-mails.</td><td>Rules are duplicated across the form, the DTO, the service and the SQL; changing a range or the name pattern means editing several places.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>Payroll history in a JSON file with a write queue</td><td>Persistent without a database; easy to inspect.</td><td>Single instance only; whole file rewritten each save.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>8</td><td>Copy employee code and name into each payroll record</td><td>History stays readable if an employee is deleted.</td><td>Copies can differ from the current employee data.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>9</td><td>Philippine tax rules isolated in one module</td><td>Rates change; one place to update and test.</td><td>Currently fixed values without effective dates.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>10</td><td>Photos owned by payroll-service, keyed by employee id</td><td>Reuses the existing AWS setup; no schema change in employee-service.</td><td>Photo is tied to an id that can change meaning; service owning images does not own employees.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>11</td><td>Upload through the back end (not directly to S3)</td><td>Server verifies file type and size; no bucket CORS; bucket never exposed.</td><td>File bytes pass through the service.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>12</td><td>Private bucket with short-lived signed links</td><td>Photos are personal data.</td><td>Links must be regenerated; extra signing work per list.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>13</td><td>OpenAI with structured output and temperature 0, plus fixed rules</td><td>Predictable machine-readable results; explainable rules; fallback when OpenAI is down.</td><td>Cost and latency per call; data leaves the machine; model output is advisory.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>14</td><td>Chat data is fetched server-side on every question; totals computed in code</td><td>Browser cannot alter the data; language models are unreliable at adding long lists.</td><td>More traffic per question; larger prompts.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>15</td><td>"Only answer from the data" enforced by instructions</td><td>Practical and effective with temperature 0.</td><td>Not a hard guarantee; needs tests with off-topic and injection questions.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>16</td><td>Accounts and password hashes owned by employee-service, which issues the tokens</td><td>One place holds the accounts, so there is no second user list to keep in sync.</td><td>Sign-in is unavailable while employee-service is down. A restart clears accounts created by sign-up.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>17</td><td>Signed JWT tokens (HS256) verified locally by every service, replacing random tokens that were looked up in a database table</td><td>No call per request, so it is faster and the services stay independent; users who already hold a token keep working while employee-service is down; no session table; the standard format other tools understand.</td><td>A token cannot be cancelled before it expires (logout, password reset and role changes do not affect tokens already issued). A leaked <span class="code">JWT_SECRET</span> lets anyone forge any user, and the same secret sits in three places.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>18</td><td>One shared secret (HS256) instead of a public/private key pair (RS256)</td><td>Simplest to set up for a local demo: one value, no key files.</td><td>Every service that can verify can also forge. Asymmetric keys would let the other two services verify only.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>19</td><td>Short token lifetime (60 minutes) as the main control over stolen tokens</td><td>Limits how long a copied token is useful without needing a server-side list.</td><td>Users must sign in again every hour; there is no refresh token.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>18</td><td>Reset code written to the server log</td><td>No e-mail service exists in the demo.</td><td>Anyone who can read the log can reset any password. A real system must send the code by e-mail.</td></tr>
</table>

<!-- ===================== 11. GAPS ===================== -->
<h1>11. Gaps and Proposed Target Architecture</h1>
<p>This section is a proposal for evolving the system. None of it is built.</p>

<h2>11.1 Gaps in the current architecture</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:26%">Area</th><th>Gap</th><th style="width:12%">Risk</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Security</td><td>JWT sign-in and two roles exist, but traffic is plain HTTP; accounts are in memory; the seeded admin password is weak; tokens cannot be cancelled before they expire; one shared secret signs and verifies; no e-mail confirmation or delivery; no general rate limiting or audit trail.</td><td>High</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Data integrity</td><td>In-memory employees; IDs reset on restart; no cross-service identity guarantee.</td><td>High</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Scalability</td><td>employee-service and payroll-service are single-instance by design.</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Quality</td><td>Almost no automated tests (only a check that employee-service starts); features are verified by hand; no automated checks that the chat refuses off-topic questions.</td><td>High</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Operations</td><td>No containers, CI/CD, centralised logging, metrics or tracing; manual start-up.</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>Privacy</td><td>Chat sends names, e-mails and phone numbers to a third party.</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Domain accuracy</td><td>Statutory rates are fixed demonstration values without effective dates.</td><td>High</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11.2 Proposed target architecture</h2>
<table>
  <tr><td colspan="5" class="ui"><b>Browser</b> - React (TypeScript), built and served as static files over HTTPS</td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="5" class="arrow">&#9660; HTTPS</td></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="5" class="svc"><b>API gateway / reverse proxy</b> - TLS termination, CORS, rate limiting, request logging, JWT verification</td></tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="arrow" style="width:20%">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow" style="width:20%">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow" style="width:20%">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow" style="width:20%">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow" style="width:20%">&#9660;</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="svc"><b>Identity provider</b><br><span class="small">OIDC, roles</span></td>
    <td class="svc"><b>employee-service</b><br><span class="small">stateless, containers</span></td>
    <td class="svc"><b>payroll-service</b><br><span class="small">stateless, containers</span></td>
    <td class="svc"><b>ai-service</b><br><span class="small">stateless, containers</span></td>
    <td class="svc"><b>Notification / PDF payslip</b><br><span class="small">optional</span></td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td><td class="arrow">&#9660;</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td class="store"><b>User store</b></td>
    <td colspan="2" class="store"><b>PostgreSQL</b> - employees and payroll runs, migrations, stable employee identity, foreign keys</td>
    <td class="ext"><b>OpenAI</b> (or self-hosted model); personal data redacted</td>
    <td class="store"><b>S3</b> - photos and payslips</td>
  </tr>
  <tr><td colspan="5" class="zone">Platform: Docker images &bull; CI/CD pipeline (build, lint, test) &bull; secrets manager &bull; central logs, metrics and tracing &bull; health checks on every service</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>11.3 Migration steps in priority order</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:36%">Step</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Replace H2 with PostgreSQL and move payroll runs into it</td><td>Persistent data, stable ids, safe concurrent access, multi-instance deployment.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>2</td><td>Move users to the persistent database; require a strong password for the admin; send reset codes by e-mail and confirm e-mail addresses at sign-up; add HTTPS; move the signing secret to a secrets manager; consider asymmetric keys (RS256) so only employee-service can sign; add a revoked-token list or refresh tokens so sign-out and password reset take effect at once; consider an external identity provider (OIDC)</td><td>Accounts survive restarts; no weak default credentials; tokens protected in transit; compromising one service no longer allows forging tokens; sign-out becomes real.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>Add automated tests: unit tests for validation and deductions, API tests per service, chat refusal tests, a UI smoke test</td><td>Safe changes and evidence the data-only chat rule holds.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>4</td><td>Make contribution and tax tables configurable with effective dates</td><td>Correct historical payslips and rate updates without code changes.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>5</td><td>Containerise, add CI/CD, secrets manager, HTTPS</td><td>Repeatable, secure deployment.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>6</td><td>Add logging, metrics, tracing and health endpoints everywhere (including employee-service)</td><td>Operational visibility.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>7</td><td>Redact personal data before it goes to OpenAI, or offer an offline option; add rate limits and usage caps</td><td>Better privacy and cost control.</td></tr>
  <tr class="alt"><td>8</td><td>Split the front end into components and API modules, adopt TypeScript</td><td>Maintainability.</td></tr>
</table>

</div>
</body>
</html>
